{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092248,"nonce":"cj6kdbe5gFjlHtfEqjQq09k++4Y=","title":"Two comments claim shipped features are unimplemented; one is an ignored test stub that should now be written","message":"Low severity, high misleading-ness. Two places in the tree assert that\nfunctionality is DEFERRED or does not exist when it demonstrably shipped. The\nrepo already has a memory note about this exact failure pattern\n(`scarab-cas-prune-lesson`: the `outputs:` \"blocked on CAS sub-tree\naddressing\" deferral was a **false premise**), and the comment that caused it\nis still in the tree telling the next reader the same wrong thing.\n\nVerified at `bd30a71`. Not part of any ADR-0061 slice.\n\n---\n\n\n`crates/scarab-engine/src/scheduler.rs:1762-1772` still says:\n\n\u003e DEFERRED — the sibling `outputs:` per-PATH publishing selection\n\u003e (`scarab_pipeline::StepSpec::outputs`) is NOT honored here. … Restricting\n\u003e the published snapshot to an authored path subset needs CAS *sub-tree*\n\u003e addressing … which scarab-storage-s3 does not have (it is whole-file /\n\u003e whole-tree only). Until that lands, `outputs:` is authored + validated in\n\u003e the pipeline spec but deliberately not consumed: it is a no-op, NOT\n\u003e silently narrowing. Blocked on CAS sub-tree addressing; see\n\u003e docs/followups.md. Do not wire this half without that support.\n\nIt is not a no-op. Per-path `outputs:` shipped via `prune_tree`\n(`crates/scarab-storage/src/lib.rs`, called from\n`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs`), which is exactly the sub-tree\naddressing the comment says does not exist. The premise (\"the port primitives\ncan't do this\") was never checked against the port.\n\nFix: delete the DEFERRED paragraph and describe what actually happens —\nincluding where the pruning is applied and what `outputs:` narrowing means\nfor the recorded root hash. Also check `docs/followups.md` still points at\nthis as open.\n\n\n`crates/scarab-e2e/tests/cas_restart_fail_fast.rs:8` says:\n\n\u003e Write once `drive_workspace` grows the fail-fast (tracked in the\n\u003e helm-dogfood follow-ups) — today this would pin the broken behaviour.\n\n`drive_workspace` already has the fail-fast:\n`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:428-436` maps\n`StorageError::NotFound` on a workspace input to\n`DriveErr::InputMissing(\"workspace input {root} is missing from the object\nstore — cannot …\")`, and there is a second site at `:1121` with the same\n\"Permanent: an input workspace is gone from the object store\" treatment.\n\nSo the test's stated blocker is gone, and the test is still an `#[ignore]` +\n`unimplemented!()` stub:\n\n```rust\nasync fn rerun_after_cas_loss_fails_fast() {\n    require_e2e!();\n    unimplemented!(\"scenario 6 not yet implemented\");\n}\n```\n\nThis is the scenario that covers the real helm-dogfood escape (CAS on an\n`emptyDir`, server restart wipes every workspace, reruns of older Runs hang\nat `Init:1/3` and dead-letter minutes later). It is worth writing now:\ncomplete a run whose step produces a workspace, wipe the CAS backing store,\nrerun a step that consumes it, assert the run fails fast with a legible\nerror rather than hanging.\n\nNote the interaction with ADR-0061 s1: the workspace service's PV removes the\n*cause* of that escape, but the fail-fast is the guard for every other way a\nCAS input can go missing (retention expiry, a pruned tree, a wrong bucket),\nso the test does not become redundant.\n\n\nA comment that says \"blocked on X\" is read as a decision, not as a stale\nnote. Both of these actively cost time: the first already produced a\nfalse-premise deferral that had to be discovered twice, and the second keeps\na known-escape scenario permanently unrun behind a reason that no longer\nholds.","files":null}]}